23 0 obj
<< /A 238 0 R /Alt (Diagram outlining four functions of self-harm: generating feeling, escaping emotion, expressing distress, and avoiding demands, separated by reinforcement type and valence. Data sources feed into a classifier model producing a one-dimensional risk score, with a note warning that functional heterogeneity is oversimplified by a single risk number and that this alone cannot determine treatment.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 20 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 239 0 R /Alt (Diagram compares the Classification Paradigm and Interactive Paradigm for risk monitoring. The Classification Paradigm uses sequential steps: input signals, one-shot classifier model, risk labeling, endpoint check, and observer role. The Interactive Paradigm involves iterative ask-listen-refine cycles, functional exploration, trajectory-based monitoring \(showing constructive and destructive trajectories\), and a dialogue partner role. Multimodal signals like facial expression, voice prosody, and behavioral sensing are highlighted as progressive for both paradigms.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 15 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 240 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a four-layer conversational AI safety system with human oversight, showing multimodal inputs, core processing, real-time trajectory monitoring, and escalation pathways for risks, including governance elements like guardrails, audit logs, and privacy controls.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 16 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
